"likma Pharmaceuticals USA Inc. v. Amarin Pharma, Inc., 608 U.S. ___, 146 8.Ct. 1391, 225 L.Ed.2d 159 (2026). Amarin Pharma, Inc., sued Hikma Pharmaceuticals USA Inc., which manufactured a generic version of a drug for which Amarin held a patent and manufactured the brand-name version. Although such generic manufacturing was allowed under federal law, a generic manufacturer like Hikma could be liable under federal patent law if it “actively induced” 1edical providers to use its generic version in ways that were still covered by the Amarin’s patent. Amarin alleged that Hikma had actively induced others to infringe Amarin’s patent for using the drug to reduce certain kinds of cardiovascular risk (the “CV indication” method of use), pointing to statements made on Hikma’s “skinny label,” on its website, and in ress releases. The district court dismissed Amarin’s complaint for failure to state a claim, but the Court of Appeals for the Federal Circuit reversed, concluding that it was “ ‘at least plausible that a physician could read’ the relevant statements ‘as an instruction or encouragement to’ infringe.” In a unanimous opinion by Justice Jackson, the Supreme Court found that the complaint failed to state an active-inducement claim and reversed the Federal Circuit. Citing and quoting Bell Atlantic Corp. v. Twombly, 550 U.S. 544, 127 8.Ct. 1955, 167 L.Ed.2d 929 (2007), and Ashcroft v. Iqbal, 556 U.S. 662, 678, 129 S.Ct. 1937, 173 L.Ed.2d 868 (2009), she rote: In order to proceed to discovery, a plaintiff must “state a claim to relief that is plausible on its face.” * * * That plausibility standard “asks for more than a sheer possibility that a defendant has acted unlawfully.” * * * Instead, to nudge a claim “across the line from conceivable to plausible,” a plaintiff must plead facts that, if true, “allo[w] the court to draw the reasonable inference that the defendant is liable for the misconduct alleged,” * * * and 521 to rule out “obvious alternative explanation{s]” for the defendant’s conduct * * *. (brackets in original). Justice Jackson emphasized that, as a matter of substantive law, a defendant is liable for the kind of claim asserted by Amarin only if it “took active steps to encourage direct infringement.” (ellipses and internal quotation marks omitted). “Most fundamentally, ‘inducement must involve the taking of affirmative,’ as opposed to passive, ‘steps to bring about the esired result’ of patent infringement.” Accordingly, “the question is not merely whether the statements could be plausibly understood to induce infringement, but whether they plausibly constitute ‘affirmative steps to bring about the desired result’ ” of infringing uses. (brackets and internal quotation marks omitted). The Court found that Amarin’s complaint “fails to allege ‘more than a sheer possibility’ that Hikma actively induced infringement of Amarin’s CV-indication patents.” Among other things, Justice Jackson noted that several of the statements by Hikma that Amarin pointed to as examples of active inducement “have an ‘obvious alternative explanation.’ ” For to oP xample, although “Hikma’s label omitted the CV Limitation of Use,” that was because the law required (with exceptions not relevant here) that “Hikma’s label must be identical to Amarin’s.” Other actions—such as the fact that Hikma described its drug as the “generic equivalent” of Amarin’s brand-name drug—were consistent with “normal industry practice.” nd other theories of active inducement were impermissibly based on “vague” statements by Hikma or on “mere omissions, inactions, or nonfeasance” that could not constitute active inducement. Accordingly, the Court concluded: “Amarin has failed to state a claim for active inducement * * * so its complaint cannot withstand Hikma’s Rule 12(b)(6) motion.” ederal Communications Commission v. AT&T, Inc., 608 U.S. , 146 S.Ct. 1418, 225 L.Ed.2d 118 (2026). In an eight-to-one decision, the Supreme Court held that the Communications Act’s enforcement structure, which allows the Federal Communications Commission (FCC) to investigate parties in an administrative process without a jury, does not violate the Seventh Amendment. Chief Justice Roberts’ opinion for the Court explained that the Seventh .mendment “requires only that, before legal rights and obligations are conclusively ‘ascertained and determined,’ * * * a party has the chance to insist that a jury make the ‘ultimate determination of issues of fact.’ ” Accordingly, the Seventh Amendment allows “nonjury adjudications making initial findings of fact that are subject to de novo review in a subsequent ry trial.” Although the Communication Act’s administrative process can lead to a “forfeiture order” that assesses a “penalty,” Chief Justice Roberts found that such orders “do not definitively resolve the parties’ legal obligations” and that the FCC’s “factual findings are not conclusive.” An FCC forfeiture order itself does not “create an obligation to pay.” And it is not the ultimate determination of any fact because, before the carriers could have been made to pay, the Government was required to prove its case to a jury.” These features distinguished the FCC proceedings from the ones at issue in Securities and Exchange Commission v. Jarkesy, 603 U.S. 109, 144 S.Ct. 2117, 219 L.Ed.2d 650 (2024). In Jarkesy, the penalties nposed “were immediately enforceable” and “the ultimate determination of the facts giving rise to the obligation to pay rested not with a jury, but with the SEC alone.” Justice Thomas authored a dissenting opinion. He did not disagree with the majority’s interpretation of either the statutory enforcement scheme or the Seventh Amendment. But Justice Thomas argued against the majority’s decision “not [to] grant the carriers any relief” in light of his view that the FCC’s prior position was that it could enforce its forfeiture rders without a de novo proceeding before a jury. He therefore urged that the carriers who had paid the assessed pena